  "dom_id": "step-q344901",
  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934289/assets/2d98be8a4f23dd7c8708fd6b9b5b5956b58ceb33bf5223c6eee5222735ccc10e.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=h(x)$ are given above. Find ${p}^{\u2032}(0)$ where $p(x)=f(x)h(x).$\n\n${p}^{\u2032}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{p}^{\u2032}(x)={f}^{\u2032}(x)\\cdot h(x)+f(x)\\cdot {h}^{\u2032}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{p}^{\u2032}(0)={f}^{\u2032}(0)\\cdot h(0)+f(0)\\cdot {h}^{\u2032}(0).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(0)=-1$ and $h(0)=-3.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-2,1)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${f}^{\u2032}(0)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $h(x)$ on $x\\in (-2,1)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${h}^{\u2032}(0)=-1.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{\u2032}(0) & ={f}^{\u2032}(0)\\cdot h(0)+f(0)\\cdot {h}^{\u2032}(0) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-3)+(-1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
